🧬 What happens right after mapping?

When you run a mapper like BWA, it processes your reads one by one in the order they appear in the FASTQ file.

Example FASTQ order:
read_1
read_2
read_3
read_4
...

BWA takes:

read_1 → aligns it somewhere in genome
read_2 → aligns it somewhere else
read_3 → another location

👉 It does NOT rearrange them after alignment

🔀 What the output looks like (unsorted BAM/SAM)

Even though reads map to very different positions, the output stays in input order:

read_1 → chr5:2000
read_2 → chr1:100
read_3 → chr3:500
read_4 → chr1:150

👉 Notice:

chr5 → chr1 → chr3 → chr1
completely random genomic order

This is what we mean by:

“stored in the order they were processed”

❗ Why does BWA do this?

Because:

it’s faster
it avoids holding everything in memory
it streams results directly to output

👉 Rearranging (sorting) is a separate, expensive step

📍 What we want instead (after sorting)

After sorting, the same reads become:

chr1:100   (read_2)
chr1:150   (read_4)
chr3:500   (read_3)
chr5:2000  (read_1)

Now:

all reads are grouped by chromosome
ordered by position
👉 Sorting organizes reads by genomic position so that indexing, fast access, and downstream analyses work correctly and efficiently.

